export const SERVER_INSTRUCTIONS = "Market data and research from the public sources themselves. Before answering with a company's reported figures, check them with verify_claims: each comes back confirmed, contradicted (with the filed figure and what the wrong one matches) or not reported. Across the market: screen_companies ranks every US-listed company on fundamentals, company_report gives one company's financials, segment_revenue its revenue by segment, product and region, valuation, risk, insiders and filings in one call, event_study measures price reactions to earnings or insider trades, mentions_trend counts a phrase in filings over time. Analyst work: financial_statements gives the income statement, balance sheet and cash flows exactly as filed (several years joined, restatements and splits shown, never mixed); filing_tables finds the table that holds a figure in a filing, or follows it through several years of filings (members, stores, ARPU, guidance tables); earnings_release reads a results press release: results, guidance as ranges, and last quarter's guidance against this quarter's results; release_check sets every figure in a results release against the 10-Q or 10-K filed after it and lists what changed; filing_check reads a 10-K or 10-Q twice, as a person sees it and as its inline XBRL tags it, and lists where the two disagree (a figure tagged at another scale than its table states, a fact tagged twice with different values, a sum that does not add up, a fact SEC's company facts hold differently). Companies: company_profile, list_filings, then read_filing (section \"risk factors\", \"md&a\" and so on for a 10-K or 10-Q; document \"EX-99.1\" for an 8-K press release); search_filings finds words across all filings; executive_pay reads a proxy's pay-versus-performance table; company_events gives a company's 8-K news (or, by event, the whole market's: restatements, bankruptcies, cyber incidents); latest_filings is what has just been filed, across every company, by form and event; filing_diff shows what changed in a 10-K's risk factors (or any item) since the last one; filing_drift follows a section over several years (how much its wording moved, hedging, readability, and a theme such as \"supply chain\"), flagging the years and themes that moved most; news gives the world's press coverage of a company or topic, with its tone; explain_move puts a day's move beside the filings and headlines around it; portfolio_xray opens a portfolio's funds into the companies they hold; comps sets a company beside its industry peers; debt_profile gives its debt, when it falls due and what it costs; ownership_breadth counts the institutions holding it, quarter by quarter; forensic_scores runs the Beneish, Piotroski and Altman screens on its filings; default_risk gives the chance it reports a bankruptcy within a year of its last annual report (a Canli model, tested walk-forward against Ohlson's O and Altman's Z''); reverse_dcf solves for the growth its share price implies; shareholder_returns what it has paid its owners each year (dividends, buybacks, the share count, payout ratios, its dividend record and yields); buybacks its share repurchases by fiscal year or quarter (cash paid, shares bought, the average price, the program authorized and remaining where tagged, the share count's change), each figure with its filing; comment_letters gives what the SEC questioned in its filings and how it answered; audit_signals its going-concern doubts, auditor changes and critical audit matters, or across the market the companies that newly raised a going-concern doubt or changed auditor; federal_contracts what the US government paid it (USAspending); drug_pipeline a drug company's active trials by phase, its next readouts and its FDA approvals; bank_financials a US bank's quarterly call-report condition (uninsured deposits, bad loans, capital ratios) or the latest bank failures (FDIC). earnings_calendar gives a company's results dates and the next one estimated, or who reports in a window; earnings_move the move options price for its next results beside its shares' past reactions; earnings_season how the S&P 500's reporting season is going (how many reported, the share that beat the consensus, the median surprise, by sector, who is due). Ownership: insider_trades (Form 4), planned_insider_sales (Form 144, filed before an insider sells) and fund_holdings (a manager's 13F); large_holders (13D/13G: 5 percent holders and activists), investment_advisers (Form ADV); funds: fund_portfolio (what a mutual fund or ETF holds, its flows and returns, from N-PORT); private markets: private_offerings (Form D raises by startups and funds), ipo_pipeline (S-1/F-1 registrations and priced IPOs) and lockup_calendar (when IPO lock-ups end, read from each final prospectus); positioning: short_interest, margin_debt (FINRA's monthly margin statistics), options_sentiment (Cboe's daily market-wide put/call ratios), off_exchange_volume (FINRA dark pools), fails_to_deliver (SEC) and futures_positioning (CFTC Commitments of Traders). Rates and the economy: economic_calendar (when the data comes out), fomc (the Fed's decisions, votes and projections), fed_speeches (what Board members said lately, a speech's passages on policy), central_bank_speeches (central bankers' speeches everywhere, from the BIS), rate_expectations (what fed funds futures price for each coming FOMC meeting: the odds of a hike, hold or cut and of each target range, by FedWatch's method), fed_liquidity (the reverse repo and standing repo facilities day by day, the Fed's securities and their runoff, reserves, the TGA and net liquidity), money_markets (overnight repo by segment against the Fed's rates, what money market funds hold and whom they lend to, primary dealers' Treasury positions), treasury_yields, treasury_auctions, federal_debt, treasury_holders (who holds Treasuries, by country, from the TIC system), economic_series (FRED), inflation_nowcast (the Cleveland Fed's nowcast of CPI and PCE inflation for the months not yet published, and its record), inflation_breakdown (CPI by component: shelter, core goods and services, energy, food and more), gdp_nowcast (the Atlanta Fed's GDPNow for this quarter: each update, what each part of spending adds, its record), recession_watch (the yield-curve model, the Sahm rule, the curve, claims and more, each against its line), financial_conditions (how tight financial conditions are and how much stress markets show: the OFR's stress index, the NFCI, credit spreads by rating, the excess bond premium and the VIX, each against its own history), state_economy (a US state beside the nation, or every state ranked), consumer_expectations (the New York Fed's survey of what households expect: inflation, prices, earnings, jobs, stocks), world_series (a country's inflation, unemployment, GDP growth, policy rate, 10-year yield or exchange rate from its own statistics office or central bank, up to 30 countries side by side, any series by its publisher's code, and check: true to compare a second official publisher), world_economy (World Bank, every country), labor_market (the US jobs report in one answer: payrolls, unemployment, wages, JOLTS, claims), job_postings (Indeed's daily job postings index in eleven economies, by sector and region), reserve_currencies (the IMF's COFER: each currency's share of the world's foreign exchange reserves), housing_market (US home values from Zillow and listings from Realtor.com, nationally or by metro, with the mortgage rate) and economic_outlook (OECD projections). Beyond the US: european_filings (a company's ESEF annual report, the figures it tagged), reference_rates (SOFR, EFFR, €STR, ECB and Bank of England rates from the central banks, and about forty central banks' policy rates), prediction_markets (Polymarket and Kalshi prices for an outcome) and crypto_fundamentals (DefiLlama: a protocol's value locked, fees and revenue, a chain's value locked, a stablecoin's supply) and funding_rates (perpetual funding and its annualized carry, OKX). Commodities: commodity_prices (the World Bank's Pink Sheet: oil, gas, metals, grains, fertilizers and more, monthly since 1960), crop_outlook (the USDA's WASDE balance sheets for grains, oilseeds and cotton by country, and this month's revisions) and energy_report (the EIA's weekly US oil and gas report: crude, Cushing, gasoline and distillate stocks against the five-year average, refinery utilization, production, trade and demand, and natural gas in storage). Compute: compute_prices (what an hour of each data-centre GPU costs on demand across the clouds, and the Cirrun GPU Index). Options: options_chain, volatility_surface and futures_curve (Cboe delayed data). The market at a glance: market_overview (US indices and the VIX, the eleven sectors ranked by the month, Treasury yields, the dollar, oil, gold, bitcoin and markets abroad, each over a day, a week, a month, three months, the year to date and a year) and market_breadth (how many S&P 500, S&P 400, Dow or sector members are above their 50- and 200-day averages, rising, at 52-week highs or lows, and the average member against the index, by sector). Prices: price_history for stocks in the US and abroad, currencies, futures, indices and crypto (no key needed; the user's Alpaca or Tiingo key is used for US stocks when set). Every result has its source URL; cite it.";
